from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much Japan do you already know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Japan is an archipelago. How many large main islands carry most of its land and people?","options":["Three","Four","Seven","Ten"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The four main islands are Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, and Shikoku, among thousands of smaller ones."},{"prompt":"The indigenous religion of Japan, centered on kami (sacred spirits in nature and ancestors), is called…","options":["Buddhism","Shinto","Confucianism","Daoism"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shinto is native to Japan; Buddhism arrived later from the Asian mainland and the two often coexist."},{"prompt":"The Tale of Genji, often called the world''s first novel, was written in the Heian court by…","options":["Matsuo Basho","Murasaki Shikibu","Natsume Soseki","Sei Shonagon"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Murasaki Shikibu, a court lady, wrote it around the year 1000; Sei Shonagon wrote the roughly contemporary Pillow Book."},{"prompt":"For centuries Japan was governed in the emperor''s name by a supreme military ruler holding the title of…","options":["Shogun","Daimyo","Ronin","Samurai"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The shogun was the top general; daimyo were regional lords and samurai were the warrior class beneath them."},{"prompt":"The 1868 event that ended the shogunate and restored the emperor as head of a modernizing state is the…","options":["Onin War","Meiji Restoration","Taika Reform","Boshin Treaty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Meiji Restoration launched rapid industrial and political modernization."},{"prompt":"A traditional haiku follows which pattern of sound units across its three phrases?","options":["5-7-5","7-5-7","5-5-5","3-4-3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The classic haiku uses 5, then 7, then 5 sound units (on), often with a seasonal reference."},{"prompt":"Washoku, the traditional Japanese cuisine recognized by UNESCO, is built around…","options":["Rice, soup, and seasonal side dishes","Deep-fried meat only","Bread and cheese","Heavily spiced curries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Washoku emphasizes rice with soup and small side dishes, freshness, balance, and careful presentation."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The land of Japan: islands and geography', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end you will be able to describe Japan as an island nation, name its four main islands, and explain how mountains, the surrounding sea, and frequent earthquakes have shaped the way its people live and think. You will also be able to point to a few landmark places that appear again and again in Japanese art and history. Warm-up, drawing on the diagnostic: how many large main islands make up Japan, and which one holds the capital? Keep that answer in mind as you read, because almost everything in later lessons, from the ancient capitals to modern Tokyo, sits somewhere on the map you are about to learn. Geography here is not background scenery. It explains why cities crowd the coasts, why the seasons matter so much in poetry and food, and why respect for the force of nature runs so deep in the culture."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Japan is an archipelago in the western Pacific, off the eastern edge of Asia, made of thousands of islands. Four large ones carry most of the land and population. Honshu is the biggest and holds Tokyo, Kyoto, and Osaka. Hokkaido lies in the cold north. Kyushu sits in the warmer southwest, and Shikoku is the smallest of the four. Mountains cover most of the interior, so farms and cities squeeze into narrow coastal plains and river valleys. The tallest peak, Mount Fuji, is a dormant volcano and a national symbol. Because Japan rests where several tectonic plates meet, earthquakes and volcanic activity are common, and the long coastline faces tsunami. The climate ranges from snowy northern winters to humid, subtropical southern summers, with a rainy season and seasonal typhoons. Sea on every side, little flat land, and constant natural force together shaped a culture attentive to the seasons, respectful of nature, and skilled at making much of small spaces."},{"heading":"Key figures & works","text":"Think of places rather than people here. Mount Fuji, about 3,776 meters high, is the most painted mountain in Japan and the subject of famous print series. The Seto Inland Sea, enclosed by Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyushu, served for centuries as a sheltered highway for trade and travel. Honshu''s broad Kanto Plain holds Tokyo, the capital and one of the largest urban regions on Earth. The older capitals of Nara and Kyoto, also on Honshu, still preserve temples and palaces more than a thousand years old. Hokkaido is the homeland of the Ainu, an indigenous people with their own language and traditions. Far to the south, the Ryukyu Islands, including Okinawa, once formed their own kingdom with a distinct culture before joining Japan. Together these places form a mental map you will return to in every later lesson."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check yourself. 1) Name Japan''s four main islands. 2) Which island is the largest and holds the capital? 3) What is Japan''s highest mountain, and what kind of mountain is it? 4) Why do most Japanese cities sit along the coast rather than inland? 5) Name one natural hazard that is common in Japan. Answers: 1) Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, and Shikoku. 2) Honshu, home to Tokyo. 3) Mount Fuji, a dormant volcano. 4) Mountains cover the interior, so the flat, livable land lies mostly along the coasts and in river valleys. 5) Earthquakes are the classic answer; volcanic eruptions, tsunami, and typhoons also count. If you missed the islands, reread the idea section, since their names anchor the geography of every later topic."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"A first common mistake is picturing Japan as a single island. It is an archipelago of thousands of islands, with four large ones holding most of the people. A second mistake is placing Tokyo in the north on Hokkaido. Tokyo sits on Honshu, on the Kanto Plain, while Hokkaido is the cold northern island and the Ainu homeland. A third is forgetting that mountains, not plains, dominate the land, which is exactly why settlement hugs the coast. Exit ticket, answer before moving on: (a) On which island is Tokyo located? (b) What landform covers most of Japan''s interior? Answers: Tokyo is on Honshu; mountains cover most of the interior. Hold these facts steady, because the history you meet next unfolds across this same crowded, mountainous, sea-ringed land."}]}'::jsonb, 'Japanese long vowels are often marked with a line over a letter (as in Tokyo or Kyoto); in plain text they are simply spelled out, as throughout this course.', 13),
  (3, 'History I: from the Heian court to the age of the shogun', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end you will be able to trace Japan''s history from the refined Heian court, through the rise of the samurai and the military governments called shogunates, to the long peace of the Edo period under the Tokugawa family. You will be able to say who really held power at each stage and how it shifted from the emperor''s court to warrior families and back toward central order. Warm-up: what title did Japan''s supreme military rulers hold, and did they rule instead of the emperor or in his name? As you read, notice a recurring pattern. The emperor remained the symbolic head of state almost the whole time, while actual power moved among nobles, warriors, and warlords. Understanding that split between symbolic and real authority is the key to the whole story."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Classical Japan reached a cultural peak in the Heian period, from 794 to 1185, when the court settled at Heian-kyo, today''s Kyoto. Nobles prized poetry, calligraphy, and refined manners, and a distinctive court culture flourished. Over time, real power slid from the emperor''s court to provincial warrior families. From 1185 the samurai, a warrior class bound by loyalty and honor, dominated public life, and military governments ruled in the emperor''s name. The shogun was the supreme general; beneath him, regional lords called daimyo commanded their own samurai. Generations of civil war among rival lords finally ended when Tokugawa Ieyasu unified the country and founded the Tokugawa shogunate in 1603, ruling from Edo, the city now called Tokyo. The Edo period, 1603 to 1868, brought more than two centuries of peace. The Tokugawa largely closed the country to foreign contact, built roads and cities, and oversaw a lively merchant culture, though society was divided into strict classes."},{"heading":"Key figures & works","text":"Murasaki Shikibu, a lady of the Heian court, wrote The Tale of Genji around the year 1000, a landmark of world literature you will meet again. Minamoto no Yoritomo became the first shogun in 1192 and set up the Kamakura shogunate, the first lasting warrior government. The samurai followed an ethical code later summed up as bushido, the way of the warrior, stressing loyalty, courage, and honor. Tokugawa Ieyasu won the decisive Battle of Sekigahara in 1600, unified Japan, and founded the shogunate that would last over 250 years. Under that peace, Edo swelled into one of the largest cities in the world, and its townspeople supported new popular arts, including kabuki theater and ukiyo-e woodblock prints, both of which return in later lessons on the arts."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Check your grasp of the sequence. 1) In which modern city was the Heian court based? 2) What is the difference between a shogun and a daimyo? 3) Who founded the Tokugawa shogunate, and in what year? 4) What word names the code of honor the samurai followed? 5) Roughly how long did the Edo period last? Answers: 1) Kyoto, then called Heian-kyo. 2) The shogun was the single supreme military ruler for all Japan; a daimyo was a regional lord who commanded his own samurai and answered to the shogun. 3) Tokugawa Ieyasu, in 1603, ruling from Edo. 4) Bushido, the way of the warrior. 5) About two and a half centuries, from 1603 to 1868. If the shogun-and-daimyo distinction slipped, reread the idea section before the next lesson."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"A frequent mistake is thinking the shogun replaced the emperor. In fact the emperor stayed on as symbolic head of state throughout, while the shogun held the real military and political power in the emperor''s name. Another mistake is treating samurai, daimyo, and shogun as the same rank. They form a ladder: samurai are the warrior class, daimyo are lords who command samurai, and the shogun stands above all the daimyo. A third is imagining the Edo period as warlike, when it was in fact a long stretch of peace and closed borders. Exit ticket: (a) Who held symbolic authority during the shogunates? (b) Which family founded the shogunate that ruled from Edo? Answers: the emperor held symbolic authority; the Tokugawa family founded the Edo shogunate. Next you will see how this closed, orderly world was forced open."}]}'::jsonb, 'Japanese personal names traditionally give the family name first; this course writes rulers such as Tokugawa Ieyasu in that order, with Tokugawa the family name.', 14),
  (4, 'History II: Meiji, the twentieth century, and postwar Japan', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end you will be able to explain how Japan went from a closed society under the shogun to a modern industrial nation, describe its path through the twentieth century including the Second World War, and summarize how postwar Japan rebuilt into a democratic economic power. You will be able to place key dates in order and connect them to the earlier shogunate era. Warm-up: what was the name of the 1868 change that ended the shogunate and restored the emperor to the center of government? This lesson is about transformation at great speed. Within a single lifetime Japan built railways, factories, and a modern state, then passed through war and defeat into a new democratic and prosperous era. Watch how each stage reacts to the one before."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In 1853 American warships under Commodore Matthew Perry arrived and pressed Japan to open to foreign trade, ending the Tokugawa policy of isolation. In 1868 the Meiji Restoration abolished the shogunate and restored the emperor as the symbol of a modernizing state. Japan swiftly built railways, factories, schools, a modern army, and a constitution, becoming an industrial power within a generation. In the early twentieth century it expanded as an empire across parts of Asia. During the 1930s and 1940s it took part in the Second World War, which ended in 1945 after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki and Japan''s surrender. Under postwar Allied occupation, Japan adopted a new constitution in 1947 that renounced war and established a parliamentary democracy with the emperor as a symbol of the nation. From the 1950s onward the country achieved a remarkable economic recovery, becoming one of the world''s largest economies, known worldwide for cars, cameras, and electronics."},{"heading":"Key figures & works","text":"Emperor Meiji gave his name to the restoration and the era of rapid modernization. Commodore Matthew Perry''s arrival in 1853 is the usual marker for the opening of Japan. The Meiji Constitution of 1889 created a modern, if limited, framework of government, while the postwar constitution of 1947 established democracy and the famous peace clause renouncing war. Symbols of the postwar recovery include the Shinkansen, the high-speed bullet train that began running in 1964, and the Tokyo Olympic Games of the same year, both signaling Japan''s return to the world stage. The rapid growth of these decades is often called an economic miracle. Together these milestones mark the arc from an isolated shogunate to a modern democratic society, and they set the stage for the cultural confidence and global influence explored later in the course."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Put the century in order. 1) Who forced Japan to open to trade in 1853, ending isolation? 2) What did the Meiji Restoration of 1868 do to the shogunate and the emperor? 3) In what year did the Second World War end for Japan? 4) What did the 1947 constitution famously renounce? 5) Name one symbol of Japan''s postwar economic recovery. Answers: 1) Commodore Matthew Perry of the United States. 2) It abolished the shogunate and restored the emperor as the symbol of a modern state. 3) 1945. 4) War, through the peace clause of the new constitution. 5) The Shinkansen bullet train or the 1964 Tokyo Olympics, among others. If the dates blur together, reread the idea section and fix 1853, 1868, 1945, and 1947 in memory before the checkpoint."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"One common mistake is thinking the emperor regained personal, hands-on rule in 1868. In both the Meiji and postwar systems the emperor served mainly as a symbol, while elected and appointed officials governed. Another is confusing the two constitutions: the Meiji Constitution of 1889 built a modern but limited monarchy, whereas the 1947 constitution established full democracy and renounced war. A third mistake is treating modernization and militarism as the same thing; the rapid industrial growth of the Meiji era came first, and the militarism that led into the Second World War came decades later. Exit ticket: (a) What year marks the Meiji Restoration? (b) What did the 1947 constitution renounce? Answers: 1868; war. With the historical timeline set, the next lessons turn from events to the beliefs, language, and arts that run through all of it."}]}'::jsonb, 'Era names such as Meiji, Taisho, Showa, and Heisei are tied to the reigns of emperors and are still used to date events and documents in Japan.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1: land and history', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which list correctly names Japan''s four main islands?","options":["Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, Shikoku","Honshu, Okinawa, Kyushu, Sado","Hokkaido, Shikoku, Okinawa, Awaji","Honshu, Taiwan, Kyushu, Hokkaido"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, and Shikoku are the four large islands; Okinawa and the others are smaller or not part of Japan."},{"prompt":"During the shogunates, who held symbolic authority as head of state?","options":["The shogun","The emperor","The daimyo","The samurai"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The emperor remained the symbolic head throughout, while the shogun wielded real power in the emperor''s name."},{"prompt":"Tokugawa Ieyasu is remembered for…","options":["Writing The Tale of Genji","Opening Japan to foreign trade","Founding the Edo shogunate in 1603","Leading the Meiji Restoration"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After winning at Sekigahara in 1600, he unified Japan and founded the Tokugawa shogunate, which ruled from Edo."},{"prompt":"The Meiji Restoration of 1868 is best described as…","options":["The start of the Edo period","The end of the shogunate and the restoration of the emperor","Japan''s surrender in World War II","The closing of Japan to foreigners"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It abolished the shogunate and restored the emperor as the symbol of a modernizing state."},{"prompt":"Mount Fuji, Japan''s tallest mountain, is…","options":["An active glacier","A dormant volcano and national symbol","A low coastal hill","Located on Hokkaido"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fuji is a dormant volcano on Honshu, about 3,776 meters high, and a national emblem."},{"prompt":"What ended Japan''s long policy of isolation in the 1850s?","options":["The Onin War","The arrival of Commodore Perry''s warships","The writing of the Pillow Book","The 1964 Olympics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Commodore Matthew Perry''s 1853 arrival pressured Japan to open to foreign trade."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Belief and language: Shinto, Buddhism, and the writing system', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end you will be able to describe the two great spiritual traditions of Japan, Shinto and Buddhism, and explain how they coexist in daily life. You will also be able to name the three scripts used together to write Japanese and say what each one is for. Warm-up: which of the two religions is native to Japan, and which arrived from the Asian mainland? These two topics, belief and language, sit at the heart of the culture. Spiritual practice shapes the festivals, shrines, and temples you see everywhere, while the writing system shapes the literature and arts you will study next. Neither is about rigid doctrine or grammar drills here; the aim is a clear, respectful overview you can build on, so that later references to a shrine, a Zen garden, or a line of calligraphy make immediate sense."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Two traditions shape Japanese spiritual life, often side by side. Shinto is the indigenous religion, centered on kami, the sacred spirits felt in nature, places, and ancestors. People visit shrines, called jinja, pass through torii gates, and join seasonal festivals. Buddhism arrived from the Asian mainland in the sixth century and spread widely, bringing temples, meditation, and schools such as Zen. Many Japanese take part in both without conflict, for example marking a birth with a Shinto blessing and a funeral with Buddhist rites. The Japanese language is written with three scripts used together. Kanji are characters borrowed long ago from Chinese, and each carries meaning. Hiragana and katakana are two syllabaries invented in Japan, together called kana. Hiragana handles grammar and native words, while katakana mostly writes foreign loanwords and adds emphasis. A single ordinary sentence may mix all three scripts at once."},{"heading":"Key figures & works","text":"Prince Shotoku, an early statesman, is traditionally credited with promoting Buddhism and giving it a place in the state during the early centuries of its spread. The Ise Grand Shrine is the most sacred site in Shinto, rebuilt in the same form at regular intervals for many centuries, an example of renewal as an act of devotion. Among Buddhist schools, Zen became especially influential on the arts, feeding the tea ceremony, ink painting, and garden design that appear in the next lesson. In writing, the invention of hiragana in the Heian period allowed court women, including the author of The Tale of Genji, to produce great literature in their own language rather than in classical Chinese. Katakana, kanji, and romaji, the Roman alphabet used in this course, round out the writing toolkit."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Test yourself. 1) Which religion is indigenous to Japan, and what are its sacred spirits called? 2) From where and roughly when did Buddhism reach Japan? 3) What is the gateway that marks the entrance to a Shinto shrine? 4) Name the three scripts used to write Japanese. 5) Which script is mainly used for foreign loanwords? Answers: 1) Shinto is indigenous, and its spirits are called kami. 2) Buddhism came from the Asian mainland in the sixth century. 3) A torii gate. 4) Kanji, hiragana, and katakana. 5) Katakana. If you mixed up hiragana and katakana, remember that hiragana carries native words and grammar, while katakana handles words borrowed from other languages."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"A common mistake is assuming a person must choose between Shinto and Buddhism. In practice many Japanese take part in both, drawing on each for different moments of life. Another mistake is treating kanji as an alphabet. Kanji are meaning-carrying characters, not letters for single sounds, which is exactly why the kana syllabaries are needed alongside them. A third is thinking katakana and hiragana look or work the same; they are two separate sets with different jobs. Exit ticket: (a) Name the sacred spirits at the center of Shinto. (b) Which of the three scripts carries meaning rather than sound? Answers: kami; kanji. With belief and writing in place, you are ready for the literature and arts that these traditions and scripts made possible, from court novels to Zen gardens."}]}'::jsonb, 'Romaji is the writing of Japanese words in the Roman alphabet; this course uses it throughout, which is why names and terms appear in familiar letters rather than Japanese script.', 13),
  (7, 'Words and beauty: literature, the arts, and aesthetics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end you will be able to name key works and figures of Japanese literature, describe several traditional and performing arts, and explain the aesthetic ideal of wabi-sabi that ties much of it together. You will be able to match a form to its description, for example a masked drama to Noh, or a short seasonal poem to haiku. Warm-up: how many sound units does a traditional haiku have across its three phrases? This is a rich lesson, so read the idea section as a map and the key works section as the landmarks on it. The underlying thread is a shared taste for restraint and suggestion. Japanese arts tend to leave space, honor the passing seasons, and find beauty in the simple and imperfect rather than the grand and polished, a sensibility that will recur through food, festivals, and design."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Japanese arts prize restraint, suggestion, and attention to the moment. In literature, the Heian court produced The Tale of Genji by Murasaki Shikibu, often called the world''s first novel. Poetry favors short forms, above all the haiku, a three-phrase poem in a 5-7-5 pattern of sound units, usually tied to a season. Later authors such as Natsume Soseki, Kawabata Yasunari, and Murakami Haruki carried Japanese fiction to readers worldwide. The traditional arts include the tea ceremony, a careful ritual of preparing and sharing powdered green tea; calligraphy, the art of brush writing called shodo; ukiyo-e, woodblock prints of the floating world of city pleasures; and garden design, from raked-gravel Zen gardens to strolling gardens. The classical stage offers two major forms: Noh, a slow, masked drama, and kabuki, a lively, colorful popular theater. Beneath much of this lies wabi-sabi, a quiet appreciation of simplicity, imperfection, and the passing of time."},{"heading":"Key figures & works","text":"Murasaki Shikibu wrote The Tale of Genji around the year 1000, while her contemporary Sei Shonagon wrote The Pillow Book, a collection of court observations. Matsuo Basho, in the seventeenth century, raised the haiku to an art, famous for a verse about a frog leaping into an old pond. In prints, Katsushika Hokusai created The Great Wave off Kanagawa and views of Mount Fuji, and Utagawa Hiroshige is known for his series of travel landscapes. Sen no Rikyu shaped the tea ceremony around the humble spirit of wabi. Zeami helped perfect Noh drama in the medieval period. In modern fiction, Natsume Soseki stands near the start and Murakami Haruki among the most widely translated today. Each name is a doorway into a whole tradition; recognizing them is enough for now."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Match forms to descriptions. 1) Who wrote The Tale of Genji? 2) What is the syllable-unit pattern of a haiku? 3) Which theater form is slow and masked, and which is lively and colorful? 4) What is ukiyo-e? 5) What aesthetic finds beauty in simplicity and imperfection? Answers: 1) Murasaki Shikibu. 2) 5-7-5. 3) Noh is the slow, masked drama; kabuki is the lively, colorful popular theater. 4) Ukiyo-e is the art of woodblock prints depicting the floating world of Edo-period entertainment and landscape. 5) Wabi-sabi. If Noh and kabuki traded places in your memory, picture Noh as hushed and ceremonial and kabuki as bold and theatrical, and the distinction will hold."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"A frequent mistake is counting English syllables for haiku. The 5-7-5 rule describes on, the sound units of Japanese, so English translations rarely match it exactly. Another mistake is confusing Noh with kabuki; keep Noh as the slow, masked classical drama and kabuki as the colorful popular theater. A third is treating the tea ceremony as merely drinking tea, when it is a deliberate ritual expressing the spirit of wabi, close attention, and respect. Exit ticket: (a) Which poet is most associated with perfecting the haiku? (b) Name the aesthetic of simplicity and impermanence running through these arts. Answers: Matsuo Basho; wabi-sabi. You will meet several of these terms again in the vocabulary lesson, and the aesthetic of restraint carries straight into the food and festivals ahead."}]}'::jsonb, 'Haiku count on, the sound units of Japanese, not English syllables; the familiar 5-7-5 rule describes those units in the original language.', 15),
  (8, 'Key terms of Japanese culture', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"kami","translation":"a Shinto spirit or sacred presence felt in nature, a place, or an ancestor","example":"A waterfall, an old tree, or a mountain may be honored as the home of a kami."},{"term":"torii","translation":"the gateway, often bright red, that marks the entrance to a Shinto shrine","example":"Passing under the torii signals that you are entering sacred shrine ground."},{"term":"shogun","translation":"the supreme military ruler who governed Japan in the emperor''s name","example":"Tokugawa Ieyasu became shogun in 1603 and ruled from Edo."},{"term":"samurai","translation":"a member of Japan''s warrior class, bound by a code of loyalty and honor","example":"A samurai served a daimyo and was expected to value honor above his own life."},{"term":"bushido","translation":"the way of the warrior, the ethical code guiding samurai conduct","example":"Bushido stressed loyalty, courage, self-discipline, and honor."},{"term":"kanji","translation":"characters borrowed from Chinese that carry meaning in Japanese writing","example":"A single kanji can stand for a whole word, such as mountain or river."},{"term":"haiku","translation":"a short poem of three phrases in a 5-7-5 pattern, often about a season","example":"Matsuo Basho''s haiku about a frog and an old pond is famous worldwide."},{"term":"wabi-sabi","translation":"an aesthetic that finds beauty in simplicity, imperfection, and impermanence","example":"A plain, slightly uneven tea bowl can express wabi-sabi better than a flawless one."},{"term":"ukiyo-e","translation":"woodblock prints depicting the floating world of Edo-period city life and landscape","example":"Hokusai''s Great Wave is the most famous ukiyo-e print."},{"term":"washoku","translation":"traditional Japanese cuisine, built on rice, soup, and seasonal side dishes","example":"Washoku was recognized by UNESCO for its balance, freshness, and presentation."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2: belief, language, and the arts', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In Shinto, kami are best described as…","options":["Written characters","Sacred spirits in nature, places, and ancestors","Warrior lords","Styles of theater"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kami are the sacred spirits or presences at the center of Shinto practice."},{"prompt":"Which statement about the Japanese writing system is correct?","options":["It uses a single alphabet","It combines kanji with two kana syllabaries","It uses only Roman letters","It has no characters for native words"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Japanese is written with kanji plus hiragana and katakana, often all in one sentence."},{"prompt":"The slow, masked classical drama of Japan is called…","options":["Kabuki","Noh","Bunraku puppetry","Ukiyo-e"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Noh is the slow, masked classical theater; kabuki is the livelier popular form."},{"prompt":"A traditional haiku is a short poem with which pattern of sound units?","options":["5-7-5","4-4-4","7-7-7","3-5-3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The classic haiku uses 5, then 7, then 5 sound units across three phrases."},{"prompt":"Wabi-sabi is an aesthetic that values…","options":["Flawless symmetry and shine","Simplicity, imperfection, and impermanence","Loud color and spectacle","Mass production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wabi-sabi finds beauty in the simple, imperfect, and transient."},{"prompt":"Who wrote The Tale of Genji, often called the world''s first novel?","options":["Matsuo Basho","Murasaki Shikibu","Zeami","Sen no Rikyu"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Murasaki Shikibu wrote it at the Heian court around the year 1000."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Living culture: festivals, food, and Japan in the world today', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end you will be able to describe the rhythm of the Japanese year through its seasonal festivals, explain the basics of washoku cuisine, and give examples of how modern Japanese culture reaches the rest of the world. You will be able to connect these living practices back to the aesthetics and seasons you met earlier. Warm-up: what is washoku built around, and why did it earn international recognition? This final lesson brings the course into the present and into daily life. The ideas of attention to the seasons, care in presentation, and finding beauty in the ordinary that shaped old poetry and tea now show up in a bowl of rice, a summer festival, and a hand-drawn comic. Watch for those continuities, because they tie the whole civilization together from the Heian court to the global screen."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Japanese daily life blends the old and the new. Seasonal festivals, called matsuri, mark the year: cherry-blossom viewing, or hanami, in spring; summer fireworks and Obon to honor ancestors; and the New Year, shogatsu, with visits to shrines and temples. Food is central to the culture. Washoku, the traditional cuisine recognized by UNESCO, is built on rice, miso soup, and small seasonal side dishes, with emphasis on freshness, balance, and careful presentation. Familiar dishes include sushi, sashimi, tempura, ramen, and green tea. In the modern era Japan has become a major cultural exporter. Manga, or comics, and anime, or animation, are read and watched worldwide. Video games, fashion, and product design carry Japanese ideas abroad, and words such as emoji, karaoke, and sushi have entered many languages. Alongside craft industries known for cars, cameras, and electronics, these creative exports give Japan a global cultural presence far larger than its size."},{"heading":"Key figures & works","text":"In animation, Miyazaki Hayao and his Studio Ghibli made internationally loved films, while Tezuka Osamu, often called the god of manga, created early classics such as Astro Boy and shaped the whole comics industry. In cinema, Kurosawa Akira directed widely admired films including Seven Samurai, influencing directors around the world. In food, the 2013 recognition of washoku by UNESCO highlighted dishes and customs built on rice, seasonality, and presentation. Festivals such as hanami, Obon, and shogatsu organize the emotional calendar of the year. Together with globally known companies in cars, cameras, and electronics, these figures and traditions show how Japan projects its culture outward, blending centuries-old sensibilities with modern media and design in ways the rest of the world has eagerly adopted."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Bring it together. 1) What is the Japanese word for a seasonal festival? 2) Name the spring custom of viewing cherry blossoms. 3) What three elements form the base of washoku? 4) What is the difference between manga and anime? 5) Name one Japanese filmmaker or animator known worldwide. Answers: 1) Matsuri. 2) Hanami. 3) Rice, soup, and seasonal side dishes. 4) Manga are comics, printed or drawn; anime is animation, shown on screen. 5) Miyazaki Hayao or Kurosawa Akira are strong answers, among others. If washoku slipped your mind, recall that it centers on rice with soup and small dishes, valuing freshness, balance, and presentation, the same care for the ordinary seen throughout the course."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"A common mistake is using manga and anime as the same word. Manga means comics, usually printed, while anime means animation. Another mistake is thinking washoku is defined by any single dish such as sushi; it is really a whole approach built on rice, soup, seasonal side dishes, and careful presentation. A third is treating modern pop culture as cut off from tradition, when its attention to craft, season, and detail grows straight out of older arts. Exit ticket: (a) What does matsuri mean? (b) What three elements anchor washoku? Answers: matsuri means a seasonal festival; washoku is anchored by rice, soup, and seasonal side dishes. That completes the tour, from Japan''s islands and history through its beliefs, language, and arts to the living culture it shares with the world today."}]}'::jsonb, 'Many borrowed Japanese words now live in other languages, such as sushi, karaoke, and emoji; they are written here in their everyday English spellings.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000016' and l.position = v.pos
);
